1. e4 {[%eval +0.20][%brsp e5]}  e5 {[%eval +0.24][%brsp Nf3]}  2. Bc4 {[%eval 0.00][%brsp Nf6]The Bishop's Opening, played five times by Magnus Carlsen in classical games. It fits his style, since a positional battle begins, and there is not much theory. In 2014, the very same Fabiano Caruana with Black beat Carlsen in the Sinquefield Cup.}  
Nf6 {[%eval +0.02][%brsp Nc3]}  3. d3 {[%eval +0.12][%brsp c6]}  c6 {[%eval +0.03][%brsp Nf3]}  
4. Nf3 {[%eval +0.03][%brsp d5]}  (4. Qe2 Be7 (4... d5 $1 $36 {is the best way to deal with Qe2} 
) 5. Nf3 d6 6. c3 Nbd7 7. Bb3 O-O 8. O-O a5 9. d4 a4 10. Bc2 Re8 11. Re1 Bf8 12. 
Qd1 b5 $17 {with a balanced middlegame, the reigning world champion scored a win over world's number 1 woman, Hou Yifan. 1-0 (61) Carlsen,M (2843)-Hou,Y (2654) Karlsruhe/Baden-Baden 2018} 
) 4... d5 {[%eval +0.10][%brsp Bb3]}  5. Bb3 {[%eval 0.00][%brsp Bd6]}  Bb4+ $1 {[%eval +0.26][%brsp Bd2]The main move here. Black provokes the c2-c3 advance, so White loses the option to play Nc3 at a later stage. Carlsen played 6.c3 in the aforementioned game.}  
6. Bd2 {[%eval +0.25][%brsp Bxd2]This is another way to play it.}  (6. c3 {happened in the aforementioned game, between these two players back in 2014.}  
Bd6 7. Bg5 dxe4 8. dxe4 h6 9. Bh4 Qe7 10. Nbd2 Nbd7 11. Bg3 Bc7 12. O-O Nh5 13. 
h3 $6 Nxg3 14. fxg3 Nc5 15. Bxf7+ {Black was able to stop this pseudo-initiative and converted the game on move 34}  
Kxf7 16. Nxe5+ Kg8 17. Ng6 Qg5 18. Rf8+ Kh7 19. Nxh8 Bg4 20. Qf1 Nd3 21. Qxd3 
Rxf8 22. hxg4 Qxg4 $17 { Carlsen,M (2877)-Caruana,F (2801) Sinquefield Cup 2nd 2014 (3) 0-1} 
) 6... Bxd2+ {[%eval +0.23][%brsp Qxd2]}  7. Nbxd2 {[%eval +0.12][%brsp a5]}  a5 
{[%eval +0.07][%brsp c3]Threatening the old and good bishop trap with ...a4. I   
                          only found one high-level reference in classical games 
     here,       where       White        played     8.a3.}  8. c3 {[%eval +0.05][%brsp Nbd7]}  
(8. a3 a4 9. Ba2 Nbd7 10. O-O O-O 11. Re1 Re8 12. d4 {Trying to start central play did not lead to any advantage for White:}  
exd4 13. exd5 Rxe1+ 14. Qxe1 cxd5 {Black has the isolated pawn, but has well placed pieces (...Nf8-e6 is a possible plan) and a b2-pawn to attack with ...Qb6}  
15. Nb1 $2 {too slow}  (15. Nxd4 {was possible}  Qb6 16. Nf5 Qxb2 17. Ne7+ Kf8 
18. Nxd5 Nxd5 19. Bxd5 Qd4 $11 {followed by ...Nf6} ) 15... d3 $6 (15... Nb8 $1 
16. Nxd4 Nc6 $17 { would give a nice advantage for Black} ) 16. cxd3 Nc5 17. d4 
Nce4 18. Nc3 Be6 $11 {½-½ (27) Shirov,A (2713)-Rodshtein,M (2641) Rogaska Slatina 2011} 
) 8... Nbd7 {[%eval +0.03][%brsp O-O]}  9. exd5 {[%eval -0.01][%brsp cxd5]A typical reaction, when Back develops his knight to d7. It is logical, because now Black can not play ...Nc6 anymore, an ideal move whenever the c6-pawn goes to d5.}  
cxd5 {[%eval 0.00][%brsp O-O][%cal Rb8c6][%csl Rd7]}  10. O-O {[%eval -0.03][%brsp Qc7]}  
