21. Qd4 Rb7 22. Rec1 {We have a similar position compared to the game, but here Black is in full control of the b-file and should equalise:}  
Qb6 23. Qxb6 Rxb6 24. a5 Rxb3 25. Rxc6 Rxb2 26. Rd6 $11 {Black is a pawn up, but he will soon lose it, when the endgame will be simply equal.} 
) 20... Rd7 {[%eval +0.34][%brsp Rc5]}  21. Red1 {[%eval +0.11][%brsp Ra5]}  
(21. Qxb4 $2 {doesn't make sense now:}  d4 $17) 21... h6 {[%eval +0.06][%brsp Rc5]}  
22. Rc5 $44 {[%eval +0.15][%brsp Ra5] I feel that here White wants to make small improvements, like Rdc1 and h3, and only then think about taking any decisive action. The d5-pawn is weak, and Black always has to count on the a4-pawn advancing at some point.}  
Ra5 {[%eval +0.25][%brsp Rdc1]}  23. Rxa5 {[%eval 0.00][%brsp Qxa5]}  (23. Rdc1 
$5 {would be similar} ) 23... Qxa5 {[%eval +0.03][%brsp Qe5]}  24. h3 {[%eval 0.00][%brsp Kh7]}  
Kh7 {[%eval +0.07][%brsp Rc1]}  25. Rc1 {[%eval +0.02][%brsp Qa6]}  Rc7 $2 {[%eval +0.90][%brsp Rxc7]In the post-mortem, Caruana mentioned that he simply misevaluated the following position, thinking he would have good activity with his queen, but soon he realised it was a bad idea. Now White is in full control.}  
(25... Qa6 $1 { would be a smart move, anticipating White's Rc5 followed by a5, with ...Qe2!}  
26. Rc5 Qe2 $132) 26. Rxc7 {[%eval +1.14][%brsp Qxc7]}  Qxc7 {[%eval +0.97][%brsp Qxb4]}  
27. Qxb4 $16 {[%eval +0.98][%brsp Qc1]}  Qc1+ {[%eval +1.02][%brsp Bd1]}  28. 
Bd1 {[%eval +1.04][%brsp Ba6]}  Ba6 {[%eval +1.04][%brsp Qd4]}  29. Qd4 {[%eval +1.05][%brsp Be2]We have the same position as before, without the b4-pawn. Now, compared with the middlegame, it is way more valuable to take the pawn back and start advancing the passed a and b-pawns, since they are free and very frightening! Also, there are no rooks to create direct counterplay against them.}  
Be2 {[%eval +0.85][%brsp b4]}  30. Kh2 {[%eval +0.80][%brsp Qc7]}  Bxd1 $6 {[%eval +1.39][%brsp Nxd1]}  
