77. a7 {[%eval +#11][%brsp Qa4]It is nice to see that Carlsen used the same technique always - defending the pawn from the diagonal (a5, Qc3 - a6, Qd3 - a7, Qe3). Now Black resigned, since it's impossible to avoid White giving a check somewhere and then queening that pawn. A superb win from the World Champion!} 
 1-0

[ContentType "Model Game"]
[Event "Gashimov Memorial 6th"]
[Round "8"]
[ECO "B33"]
[Guid "R3799553e8caf4c64855b490129a76df8"]
[Date "2019.04.08"]
[White "Karjakin, Sergey"]
[Black "Carlsen, Magnus"]
[WhiteElo "2753"]
[BlackElo "2845"]
[Annotator "Nielsen,Peter Heine"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Preamble "A rook pawn sacrifice with an attack on the kingside and in the center."]


1. e4 {[%eval +0.30][%brsp e5]}  c5 $5 {[%eval +0.41][%brsp Nf3]Considering the tournament standings, maybe made this a somewhat surprising move. Magnus was leading the tournament by ½ a point ahead of Karjakin who then had a further 1 point to the rest of the field. Meaning for Karjakin this was almost a " must-win" situation as if the game was drawn he would need not only to score half a point more in the final round, but also to win a playoff. Even so Magnus stayed loyal to the Sveshnikov which generally does promise solidity, but as shown in the London WC match does allow one way for White to get a very complex battle:}  
2. Nf3 {[%eval +0.33][%brsp d6]}  Nc6 {[%eval +0.32][%brsp d4]}  3. d4 {[%eval +0.29][%brsp cxd4]}  
cxd4 {[%eval +0.32][%brsp Nxd4]}  4. Nxd4 {[%eval +0.39][%brsp Nf6]}  Nf6 {[%eval +0.45][%brsp Nc3]}  
5. Nc3 {[%eval +0.37][%brsp e5]}  e5 {[%eval +0.26][%brsp Ndb5]}  6. Ndb5 {[%eval +0.33][%brsp d6]}  
d6 {[%eval +0.35][%brsp Nd5]}  7. Nd5 $5 {[%eval +0.45][%brsp Nxd5]Karjakin takes up the gauntlet and follow in Caruanas footsteps. After London Magnus won with the black pieces against Van Forest in Wijk aan Zee as well as Navara earlier in Shamkir. Magnus said after the game that he was indeed exited to see 7.Nd5 on the board as it always leads to very exiting positions. And that while the engines indeed seems to give a preference for White, people tend to underestimate the human factor and that Black's position is easier to play, as he generally tries to aim for the opponent's king, while White has to balance positional gains on the queenside with also caring about the security of his own king.}  
Nxd5 {[%eval +0.41][%brsp exd5]}  8. exd5 {[%diag 2][%eval +0.40][%brsp Nb8]}  
Ne7 $5 {[%eval +0.50][%brsp c4] The move from game 12 of the match as well as the tiebreaker and vs. Van Forest. 8...Nb8 is of equal value and was seen in game 8 & 10 as well as vs. Narvara, meaning for Karjakin it was impossible to know for sure which move to expect.}  
9. c4 {[%eval +0.50][%brsp Nf5]}  Ng6 {[%eval +0.60][%brsp Qa4]}  10. Qa4 {[%eval +0.39][%brsp Bd7]}  
Bd7 {[%eval +0.48][%brsp Qb4]}  11. Qb4 {[%diag 2][%eval +0.46][%brsp Bf5]}  Bf5 
{[%eval +0.43][%brsp Qa4]The move from game 12, later Magnus preferred 11...Qb8. 
                  Anyway this is very much a theoretical debate in progress 
where     the        status      very   much is unsettled. Actually a rare 
occurence   these   days    that     suddenly   an    oasis   of almost 
untouched but very     interesting    positions     suddenly  occurs  in a    
popular   opening     variation at the    highest level.}  12. Qa4 {[%eval +0.46][%brsp Bd7]}  
Bd7 {[%eval +0.53][%brsp Qb4]}  13. Qb4 {[%eval +0.51][%brsp Bf5]}  Bf5 {[%eval +0.51][%brsp h4]}  
14. h4 {[%eval +0.48][%brsp a6]}  h5 {[%eval +0.60][%brsp Bg5]}  15. Bg5 $5 {[%eval +0.57][%brsp Qb8]Karjakin of course comes prepared and plays the critical move. Caruana went 15.Be3 but got into trouble before the game controversially ended in a sudden draw.}  
Qb8 {[%eval +0.60][%brsp Qa5]}  16. Be2 {[%eval +0.47][%brsp a6]}  a6 {[%eval +0.46][%brsp Nc3]}  
17. Nc3 {[%diag 2][%eval +0.48][%brsp Qc7]}  Qc7 {[%eval +0.47][%brsp g3]In principle only this is the novelty, as 17...Nf4 18 Bf1 Be7 19 Bxe7 Kxe7 20 g3 Ng6 21 Qb6 was played in the game Houdini- Stockfish which Grischuk pointed out during his interesting live commentary during game 12 of the WC match. Magnus' idea, however, is very different and much more human. He does not want to leave his king vulnerable in the centre, on the contrary it will be hidden safely away before he launches an attack on the enemy king. That the price is the h5-pawn is of no significance. Black is playing for mate!}  
(17... Nf4 18. Bf1 Be7 19. Bxe7 Kxe7 20. g3 Ng6 21. Qb6 {Houdini- Stockfish.} ) 
18. g3 {[%eval +0.44][%brsp Be7]}  Be7 {[%eval +0.48][%brsp Qa4]}  19. Be3 {[%eval +0.44][%brsp Nf8]}  
e4 $1 {[%eval +0.52][%brsp Qa4]That's the key point. Playing ...Nf8-d7 and covering h5 before castling of course is possible, but this is so much more temptin, as the knight on e5 touches vital squares at f3 and d3 and starts making the "aggressive" h4 and g3 by White look like weaknesses.}  
20. O-O {[%diag 2][%eval +0.05][%brsp O-O]}  O-O {[%eval +0.08][%brsp Bxh5]}  
21. Bxh5 {[%eval +0.07][%brsp Ne5]}  Ne5 {[%eval +0.15][%brsp Be2]}  22. Be2 {[%eval +0.07][%brsp Rfe8]}  
Qd7 {[%eval +0.15][%brsp Qa4]White won a pawn, but Black immediately zooms in on the sore spots in White's position, immediately threatening 23...Bg4 followed by a deadly invasion on the f3 square.}  
23. Qa4 {[%eval +0.07][%brsp Qc8]}  Qc8 $1 {[%eval +0.14][%brsp Qd1]Obviously not allowing the exchange of queens, while still upholding the threat of ...Bg4, meaning Karjakin has to act.}  
24. c5 $6 {[%eval -0.02][%brsp dxc5]That is, however, the best White's position had on offer, as now things are only to get worse. Much worse.}  
(24. Qd1 $1 {was the right move, controlling g4. Indeed Black is perfectly fine} 
 Nxc4 {when}  25. Bxc4 Qxc4 26. Bd4 {leads to an equal position.} ) 24... dxc5 {[%eval 0.00][%brsp Nxe4]}  
25. Nxe4 {[%eval -0.18][%brsp Re8]}  c4 $5 {[%eval -0.04][%brsp Qc2]Magnus was happy with this move after the game. 25...b5 also was possible, forcing White to "self-pin" with 26 Qc2. But as the computer shows, after 25...c4 26 Qc2! is still the best move, so why "force" White in the right direction?}  
26. Nc3 {[%eval -0.80][%brsp b5]}  b5 $1 {[%eval -0.82][%brsp Qd1]Magnus said that he saw 26...Bd3 27.Bxd3 Nf3+ 28.Kg2 Nxh4+ forcing a draw by perpetual, but that while for the tournament situation this would indeed be a good result, Black's attacking options are just too tempting to pass on.}  
(26... Bd3 27. Bxd3 Nf3+ 28. Kg2 Nxh4+ $11) 27. Qd1 {[%eval -0.77][%brsp b4]}  
b4 $1 {[%eval -0.81][%brsp Na4]Another forceful move. Sure it does ruin the solidity of Black's pawnstructure, but much more importantly it forces White's knight basically as far away as possible from the kingside where the game is going to be decided, as well as it allows Black's bishop access to the e4 square.}  
28. Na4 {[%eval -0.75][%brsp Be4]}  Be4 {[%eval -0.75][%brsp Qd4]}  29. Qd4 {[%eval -0.71][%brsp Qf5]}  
(29. f3 {[%cbm If]then}  Qh3 $1 {decides.} ) 29... Qf5 {[%eval -0.42][%brsp f3]} 
 30. f4 $2 {[%eval -1.60][%assm 2][%brsp Qg6]While now with a couple of powerful moves, Magnus shows his attacking skills.}  
(30. f3 $1 {[%cbm The absolutely last chance was] when after}  Nxf3+ (30... Bxf3 
31. Rae1 $1 {and White very much fights on} ) 31. Bxf3 Bxf3 32. Qf4 {White defends.} 
) 30... Qg6 $1 {[%diag 6][%eval -1.75][%brsp Bf2]A great intermezzo. By attacking g3, Black forces White into unprotecting the f4 pawn.}  
(30... Nd3 31. g4 $1 Qh7 32. h5 {is much less clear.} ) 31. Bf2 {[%eval -1.86][%brsp Nd3]}  
Nd3 {[%eval -1.92][%brsp h5]}  32. h5 {[%eval -1.87][%brsp Qf5]}  Qf5 $1 {[%eval -1.97][%brsp Bxd3]This is the big difference, the basic point being that 33.g4 Qxf4 now is possible due to the bishop on f2 blocking the rook on f1.}  
(32... Nxf4 {might look tempting, but after}  33. Rae1 Qf5 34. Bg4 $3 {the tables are completely turned as}  
Qxg4 35. Rxe4 Ne2+ 36. Kg2 $1 {wins for White.} ) 33. Bg4 {[%eval -2.86][%brsp Qxg4]Better was 33.Bxd3, still however leading to a winning position for Black after either recapture.}  
Qxg4 {[%eval -2.81][%brsp Qxe4]}  34. Qxe4 {[%eval -3.50][%brsp Rae8]}  Bd6 {[%diag 2][%eval -3.46][%brsp Qh1]Of course threatening to take on f4, but while that is possible to prevent, the main theme is that Black's knight on d3 protects the e1-square meaning that his rooks can penetrate unopposed in the e-file.}  
35. Qg2 {[%eval -3.63][%brsp Rae8]}  Rae8 $1 {[%eval -3.80][%brsp a3]}  36. Bd4 {[%eval -4.46][%brsp Qxh5]}  
Qxh5 $1 {[%eval -4.21][%brsp Qf3]Now, however, 37...Re2 is a deadly threat as 38.Qf3 would then allow mate on h2.}  
(36... Re2 37. Qf3 $1 {would force off the queens, leaving White with some hope in an indeed bad ending.} 
) 37. Qf3 {[%eval -4.64][%brsp Qg6]}  Qg6 $1 {[%eval -4.95][%brsp Nb6]Again seemingly slow, but like in move 34 Black uses the threat to the f4 pawn to gain time for his real aims.}  
38. Kh1 {[%eval -5.54][%brsp Re4]}  Re4 $1 {[%eval -5.78][%brsp Rf2]}  39. Bf2 {[%eval -6.01][%brsp Rfe8]}  
Rfe8 {[%eval -5.97][%brsp Kh2]Blacks threats are numerous. 39...Nxf4, 39...Re2 or 39...Nxf2+ followed by 40...Re3. Karjakin had seen enough and resigned meaning Magnus won the Shamkir tournament with a round to spare, but also scored his 4th (!) win in a row vs. 7.Nd5. Similarities to Kasparov's dominance tournament-wise as well as his Kings Indian come to mind.} 
 0-1

