(29... gxf6 $5 {was a serious option, but White's position would remain defensible after}  
30. Bd2 $1 {.} ) 30. Ra3 {[%diag 2][%eval -0.38][%brsp e4]The position has remained around equal.}  
e4 $1 {[%eval -0.24][%brsp dxe4]A great practical decision. Black exchanges his bad bishop, activates his major pieces and fortifies his knight's outpost on e3.}  
31. dxe4 {[%eval -0.27][%brsp Bxc3]}  Bxc3 {[%eval -0.30][%brsp Rxc3]}  32. Rxc3 
{[%eval -0.29][%brsp Qe5]}  Qe5 {[%eval -0.18][%brsp Qd2]}  33. Rc1 {[%eval -0.32][%brsp Qd4]}  
Ra8 {[%eval -0.16][%brsp Qd2]}  (33... Qd4 $5 {was also an option. To prevent this, White could start with 33.Qd2. However,}  
34. Kh1 Ra8 35. Ba2 $1 {still maintains equality.} ) 34. h3 {[%eval -0.76][%brsp Qd4]}  
(34. Qd2 Ra1 35. Be2 {might have been more circumspect.} ) 34... h6 {[%eval -0.42][%brsp Qd2]}  
(34... Ra1 35. Rxa1 Qxa1+ 36. Kh2 h6 {looks more pleasant for Black, but White's position is solid enough to resist.} 
) 35. Kh2 {[%eval -0.48][%brsp Kh7]}  (35. Qd2 $1) 35... Qd4 {[%eval 0.00][%brsp e5]}  
36. Qe1 $6 {[%eval -0.29][%brsp Qe5]}  (36. e5 $1 Qxe5 37. Bd3 {or} )  (36. Ba2 { might still have sufficed for equality, but it is very hard to keep one's cool with the clock ticking.} 
) 36... Qb2 {[%eval -0.20][%brsp Be2]}  37. Bf1 {[%eval -1.53][%assm 2][%brsp Ra2]}  
(37. Be2 $1 {[%cbm Computers suggest]}  Ra2 38. e5 $3 {, for instance:}  Qxe5 
(38... Qxe2 $6 39. Qxe2 Rxe2 40. b6 $1 $11 { and the passed pawns save White.} ) 
 (38... dxe5 $6 39. d6 $1 cxd6 40. Rc8+ Kh7 41. Bd3+ g6 42. Rc2 $3 Qb3 (42... 
Nxc2 $4 {even loses to}  43. Qe4 $1) 43. Qh4 $1 Rxc2 44. Qe7+ Kg8 45. Qe8+ Kg7 
46. Qxg6+ Kf8 47. Qxd6+ {with a perpetual.} ) 39. b6 $1 Qg5 $1 (39... Nxg2 40. 
b7 Nxe1 41. b8=Q+ Kh7 42. Qb1+) 40. Qf2 Qg3+ $1 41. Qxg3 fxg3+ 42. Kxg3 Rxe2 43. 
bxc7 Rxg2+ 44. Kf4 Nxd5+ 45. Ke4 Nxc7 46. Rxc7 {with a drawish endgame. But this would be extremely difficult to find even in a classical game. We should not criticise Sergey for missing this. After all, most of us prefer human games to computer encounters, which might be far too incomprehensible for us mortals.} 
) 37... Ra2 {[%eval -1.59][%brsp Rb1]}  38. Rxc7 $2 {[%eval -4.98][%assm 1][%brsp Nxg2]}  
(38. Rb1 $1 {was a must. Black's position looks dominant after}  Qd4 {, but White defends everything for the time being.} 
) 38... Ra1 {[%eval -5.29][%brsp Qxa1]Magnus played an excellent game. He created such strong pressure, that even such a great defender like Sergey failed to cope with it.} 
