1. e4 {[%eval +0.19][%brsp e5]}  c5 {[%eval +0.31][%brsp Nf3]}  2. Nf3 {[%eval +0.38][%brsp Nc6]}  
Nc6 {[%eval +0.35][%brsp Bb5]Magnus favourite opening of late, obviously played in the World Championship but actually starting already from the 2018 Sinquefield Cup. There MVL played 3.Nc3, but since then the focus has been on first 3.Bb5 and then the popular 7.Nd5 in the Sveshnikov where Caruana earlier in the tournament continued his theoretical discusion vs. Magnus.}  
3. Nc3 {[%eval +0.28][%brsp e5]Svidler copies MVL, staying away from the most principal battles.}  
e5 {[%eval +0.28][%brsp Bc4]}  4. Bc4 {[%eval +0.30][%brsp d6]}  Be7 $5 {[%eval +0.31][%brsp d3] Absolutely the standard move, but in St. Louis Magnus choose the more surprising 4...g6, but here reverts to the standard.}  
5. d3 {[%eval +0.33][%brsp Nf6]}  d6 {[%eval +0.30][%brsp a4]}  6. Nd2 $5 {[%eval +0.28][%brsp Nf6] Kasparov's favourite move, aiming for the control of d5. Later attention switched to 6.0-0 Nf6 7.Ng5 leading to a much sharper battle, but the positional approach both has logic and venom.}  
Nf6 {[%eval +0.29][%brsp Nf1]The modest 6...Bg5 was the choice in the early encounters, but modern theory has started to focus on this natural development move.}  
7. Nf1 {[%eval +0.26][%brsp Bg4]}  Nd7 $5 {[%eval +0.42][%brsp Ng3]This, however, is much less played, but does follow in the footsteps of both Kramnik and Gelfand and was also employed twice by Lenic in his 2917 World Cup match vs. Magnus' French second Laurent Fressinet. The idea is to exchange White's strong knight on d5, and while obviously this also could be done from f6, and thus by logic Black just throws away 2 tempi, the point is doing it on Black's terms, trying to force White to take on b6.}  
8. Nd5 {[%eval +0.26][%brsp Nb6]}  (8. Ne3 {was Fressinet and Kasparov's choice, and while White does keep a nominal edge, neither managed to break down the solidity of Black's position.} 
) 8... Nb6 {[%eval +0.27][%brsp Nxb6]}  9. Nxb6 $5 {[%eval +0.30][%brsp axb6]}  
axb6 {[%eval +0.35][%brsp c3]}  10. c3 {[%eval +0.29][%brsp O-O]This is the point of White's play, creating a somewhat akward double pawn, and now not allowing 10.Ne3 b5! removing the weakness due to 11.Bxb5 Qa5+! losing a piece.}  
(10. Ne3 b5 $1 11. Bxb5 Qa5+ $1 {[%cal Ga5e1,Ga5b5]} ) 10... O-O {[%eval +0.27][%brsp h4]}  
11. Ne3 {[%eval +0.24][%brsp Bg5]}  Bg5 {[%eval +0.18][%brsp O-O]}  12. O-O {[%eval +0.25][%brsp Bd7]Here Kramnik took on e3 followed by ...Qe7 and Be6 equalising, and the engines verify similar thoughts, suggesting 12...Be6. But how do you actually play for a win?}  
Kh8 $5 {[%eval +0.25][%brsp a4]At least posing the question if ...f5 next is a "threat" as well as generally safeguarding the king.}  
13. a3 $6 {[%eval +0.15][%brsp f5]Svidler however indicates that ...f5 does not worry him, and Magnus follows with maybe you should be?}  
