17. Ba2 {[%eval +0.15][%brsp Rxf5]Not the battle focuses around if White's bishop on a2 will be in or out of play:}  
(17. Bb5 $5 {[%cbm  but safer would be the liquidating]}  Rxf5 18. Bxc6 {simplifying with a likely draw.} 
) 17... Rxf5 {[%eval 0.00][%brsp Qb3]}  18. Qg4 {[%eval -0.08][%brsp g6]}  Rf6 {[%eval -0.01][%brsp Rce1]}  
19. f4 $6 {[%eval -0.29][%brsp exf4]The wish to exchange as well as annulate the pressure in the f-line is understandable, but the ensuing tactics turns out (very) well for Black. Common sense chess with 19. Rce1 Qd6 20.Qd1 brings some harmony into White's positions, and while his aggressive prospects are not huge, he does have a solid position. After the game move, however, not so much.}  
(19. Rce1 Qd6 20. Qd1) 19... exf4 {[%eval -0.35][%brsp Qg5]}  20. Qg5 {[%eval -0.30][%brsp Qf8] A neccesary intermediate move}  
(20. Rxf4 $2 {[%cbm as]loses material immediately to }  Ne5 21. Qg3 Rxf4 22. 
Qxf4 Nxd3) 20... Qf8 $1 {[%diag 6][%eval -0.31][%brsp Qxd5]While the early phase of this game of Magnus has seen standard moves combined with a bit of provocation, the rest is another serious matter, developing a strong attack and finishing it off in style. Keeping the f4 pawn is crucial in his attacking concept, and readmitting the bishop on a2 into the game of much lesser importance, as the game will show its just shooting into thin air.}  
21. Qxd5 {[%eval -0.28][%brsp Rd8]21.Bxd5 rerouting the bishop to e4 would have been ideal strategically, but unfortunately drops a pieces due to 21...Rf5!}  
(21. Bxd5 Rf5 $1) 21... Rd8 $1 {[%eval -0.19][%brsp Qg5]}  22. Qf3 $6 {[%eval -0.84][%brsp Ne5]Logical and consequent, but giving Black everything he wants.}  
(22. Qg5 h6 23. Qh5 Rxd3 24. Rce1 {is indeed a pawn down, but with decent compensation with good chances of a draw.} 
) 22... Ne5 {[%eval -0.85][%brsp Qe4]}  23. Qe4 $5 {[%eval -0.92][%brsp Ng4]Svidler maybe pinned his hopes on this move, kicking the knight and thus not allowing ...f3 which would be lethal after the direct 23.Qxb7.}  
(23. Qxb7 f3 $1) 23... Ng4 $1 {[%eval -0.79][%brsp h3]Magnus is in the zone, both mentally but also with the knight invading next on e3 where it becomes the fix-point of the black attack.}  
(23... Nxd3 {would leave Black a pawn up but the attack would then evaporate.} ) 
24. Rce1 {[%eval -0.79][%brsp Ne3]}  Ne3 {[%eval -0.82][%brsp Rf2]}  25. Rf2 {[%eval -0.96][%brsp Re8]}  
Re8 $1 {[%eval -0.85][%brsp Qf3]}  26. Qxb7 {[%diag 2][%eval -1.21][%brsp g5]}  
g5 $1 {[%eval -1.22][%brsp b4]First a pawn sac and then followed up by this key move. The knight on e3 and the pawn on f4 completely block White's rooks, and the bishop on a2 basically is on an empty diagonal as Black's attack will happen on the dark squares. White's position might look sufficiently solid, but the march of the g-pawn drastically changes that. White'ss position is actually so bad that there is nothing he can do, and Svidler decides to literally do that, going back and forth:}  
27. Rfe2 $6 {[%eval -3.99][%assm 1][%brsp f3]I guess should be criticised on principle, but I cannot offer any reasonable alternative.}  
g4 {[%eval -3.78][%brsp d4]}  28. Rf2 {[%eval -4.05][%brsp Qh6]}  Qh6 $1 {[%eval -4.03][%brsp Qc7]Simply threatening ...g3 and then mate on h2.}  
29. Qc7 {[%eval -4.30][%brsp Ref8]X-raying the g3 square.}  Ref8 $1 {[%eval -4.25][%brsp Qe5]Renewing the threat of... g3, with hxg3 Ng4+! now being the followup, and there is no longer a rook hanging on e8 against which White can do absolutely nothing.}  
30. h3 $5 {[%eval -6.81][%brsp gxh3]One might think that Svidler decided to be a good sport and allow a very spectacular finish, but actually his position is just dead lost.}  
(30. Qe5 {[%cbm The computer wants tragic moves like]}  g3 31. hxg3 Ng4 32. 
Qxf6+)  (30. g3 {[%cbm or]}  fxg3 31. Qxg3 {allowing}  Rxf2 {just to illustrate how desperate the situation actually is.} 
) 30... gxh3 {[%eval -7.56][%brsp Bd5]}  31. g3 {[%eval -#13][%brsp fxg3]}  fxg3 
{[%eval -#9][%brsp Qxg3]}  32. Rxf6 $5 {[%eval -#2][%brsp h2] The idea is that after 32...Qxf6 33 Qxg3 white does fight on, but also that after:}  
h2+ $1 {[%eval -#1][%brsp Kh1]}  33. Kh1 {[%eval -#1][%brsp g2]}  g2# {[%eval #]We have a mate that just speak for itself in its beauty:} 
 0-1

