[Preamble "Attack on the king with the h-pawn march and an exchange sacrifice in the center."]


1. c4 {[%eval +0.23][%brsp e5]}  Nf6 {[%eval +0.29][%brsp d4]}  2. Nf3 {[%eval +0.30][%brsp c5]}  
g6 {[%eval +0.56][%brsp Nc3]}  3. g3 {[%eval +0.27][%brsp c6]}  Bg7 {[%eval +0.21][%brsp Nc3]}  
4. Bg2 {[%eval +0.17][%brsp c6]}  O-O {[%eval +0.27][%brsp Nc3]}  5. O-O {[%eval +0.21][%brsp c6]}  
d6 {[%eval +0.37][%brsp d4]}  6. Nc3 {[%eval +0.39][%brsp c5]}  Nc6 {[%eval +0.43][%brsp d4]}  
7. d4 {[%eval +0.37][%brsp e5]}  Bf5 {[%eval +0.55][%brsp Nh4]}  8. Bg5 {[%eval +0.35][%brsp h6]}  
Ne4 {[%eval +0.42][%brsp Nxe4]}  9. Nxe4 {[%eval +0.42][%brsp Bxe4]}  Bxe4 {[%eval +0.35][%brsp d5]}  
10. d5 {[%eval +0.31][%brsp Bxf3]}  Bxf3 {[%eval +0.34][%brsp exf3]}  11. exf3 {[%eval +0.35][%brsp Nd4]}  
Nd4 {[%eval +0.39][%brsp Re1]}  12. Re1 {[%eval +0.28][%brsp Re8]}  Re8 {[%eval +0.30][%brsp Rb1]Let's skip the opening and come to the important part of the game. Until this point both sides had played quite quickly. But here Magnus sunk into deep thought. And how deep was it? 40 minutes in all! So what exactly was going through his mind? It is clear that White has the bishop pair. But the knight on d4 is strongly placed. It is important not to let Black establish the knight on d4 with the move ...c5. Black wants to play ...c5 and reply to dxc6 with bxc6, when he can strengthen his knight with c5. Here Magnus came up with a deep idea to put his rook on e4 followed by pushing his h-pawn down the board. Quite original. For normal players like me the move Bd2 followed by Bc3 looks natural, but of course it is nowhere close to as ambitious as what Magnus goes for.}  
13. Re4 $5 {[%eval +0.21][%brsp c5]The funny thing is that this move has already been played twice by the great player Aleksander Wojtkiewicz.}  
(13. Bd2 c5 14. dxc6 bxc6 15. Bc3 c5 $11 { is some sort of a dream scenario for Black.} 
)  (13. Be3 Nf5 14. Bc1 c6 $11) 13... c5 {[%eval +0.35]}  14. dxc6 {[%eval +0.27][%brsp Nxc6]}  
Nxc6 {[%eval +0.23][%brsp Bf1]}  15. h4 $5 {[%eval +0.13][%brsp h6]Not really caring about the b2-pawn.}  
Qb6 {[%eval +0.76][%brsp Rb1]}  (15... Bxb2 16. Rb1 Bg7 17. Rxb7 {when Black is not worse, but neither is White. } 
) 16. Rb1 {[%eval +0.57][%brsp Bd4]}  a5 {[%eval +0.68][%brsp h5]}  17. Be3 {[%eval +0.61][%brsp Qd8]}  
Qc7 {[%eval +0.77][%brsp h5]}  18. h5 {[%eval +0.70][%brsp Red8]}  e5 {[%eval +0.95][%brsp Bf1]}  
19. Qd2 {[%eval +0.69][%brsp Ne7]}  Nd4 {[%eval +1.39][%brsp h6]}  20. h6 $1 {[%eval +1.37][%brsp Bf6]A superb move by Magnus, who doesn't fear his rook getting trapped in the centre of the board.}  
f5 {[%eval +2.01][%brsp Rxd4]}  (20... Bh8 21. Bxd4 exd4 22. Rxd4 Bxd4 23. Qxd4 
f6 24. Qxf6 Qe7 25. Qd4 $16 {is excellent compensation.} ) 21. Rxd4 $1 {[%eval +2.14][%brsp exd4]}  
exd4 {[%eval +2.04][%brsp Bxd4]}  22. Bxd4 {[%eval +2.08][%brsp Bxd4]}  Bf8 {[%eval +2.75][%brsp b3]}  
(22... Bxd4 23. Qxd4 f4 $1 {at least temporarily closing in the g2-bishop.}  24. 
Qxf4 Qc5 25. Qd2 $16 {[%cal Gf3f4]} ) 23. Bc3 {[%eval +2.93][%brsp Qc5][%cal Gd2d4,Gd4h8]}  
Qxc4 {[%eval +4.93][%brsp f4]}  24. f4 {[%eval +5.09][%brsp Re4][%cal Gg2d5]}  
Re4 {[%eval +5.09][%brsp b3]}  25. b3 $1 {[%eval +5.03][%brsp Qe6]}  Qc5 {[%eval +5.53][%brsp Rc1]}  
26. Ba1 $1 {[%eval +5.26][%brsp Rc8]The queen is coming to b2 and there would be no way to avert the mate.}  
d5 {[%eval +5.51][%brsp Rc1]}  27. Rc1 $1 {[%eval +5.63][%brsp Qe7]Daniil Yuffa found it futile to continue the resistance. The main point is that after the queen moves, Bxe4 follows and then Qc3 mates the black king on the long diagonal. A beautiful game by Carlsen. Particularly impressive was his idea of Re4 followed by h4, which he found over the board.}  
(27. Rc1 Qd6 28. Bxe4 fxe4 29. Qd4 Kf7 30. Qh8 $18)  1-0

