Kh7 {[%diag 2][%eval +#2][%brsp Qh6]It looks as if Black were suddenly winning, but Magnus had foreseen everything and finished the match in style.}  
50. Qh6+ $3 {[%eval +#1][%brsp gxh6]and Black resigned before an inevitable mate. It was a fascinating conclusion to a very tense match. Sergey Karjakin deserves every credit for his strong play. He and his team prepared for the match excellently and could have won it. He showed that it is possible to play against Magnus on equal terms and that the Norwegian's throne isn't unshakeable. But I also believe that Magnus deserves the title. He has been the world's number 1 for many years and he is the strongest player in the world. He did not lose in the classical part of the match and eventually prevailed in rapid chess.} 
 1-0

[ContentType "Model Game"]
[Event "Bilbao Masters 9th"]
