[Black "So, Wesley"]
[WhiteElo "2855"]
[BlackElo "2770"]
[Annotator "Carlsen,M"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Preamble "Attack on the uncastled king with the decisive control of the d-file."]


1. e4 {[%eval +0.20][%brsp e5]}  e5 {[%eval +0.31][%brsp Nf3]In round 4 this was already my third white game. In the first two games against predominantly 1...e5 players I was suprised by 1...c5 and wheeled out a couple of obscure Anti-Sicilians. I got an excellent position against Nakamura and then nothing against Karjakin and won, so naturally I was expecting to be surprised again.}  
2. Nf3 {[%eval +0.21][%brsp Nc6]}  Nc6 {[%eval +0.25][%brsp Bb5]}  3. Bb5 {[%eval +0.20][%brsp Nf6]}  
Nf6 {[%eval +0.28][%brsp O-O]Wesley also plays other systems, but an invitation to the Berlin is never a surprise from a top player.}  
4. d3 {[%eval +0.14][%brsp Bc5]In my early years as an ambitious player the Berlin was not considered a particulary good opening, and I used to grab the invitation with both hands, with excellent results. More recently however, more solid systems for Black have been found, and I'm finding myself rejecting the invitation on a regular basis.}  
Bc5 {[%eval +0.17][%brsp c3]}  5. Bxc6 {[%eval 0.00][%brsp dxc6]}  dxc6 {[%eval 0.00][%brsp h3]}  
6. Qe2 {[%eval -0.17][%brsp Qe7]}  (6. Nbd2 {[%cbm Back in 2012 I started playing]here, and to my surprise it became very popular. The text has similar ideas, but is much less well-known.} 
) 6... Qe7 {[%eval -0.07][%brsp a3]}  7. Nbd2 {[%eval -0.21][%brsp a5]}  Bg4 {[%eval +0.02][%brsp Nc4]Naturally, there are plenty of other options on this and the previous move, but this plan is always decent. The bishop will temporarily be misplaced on h5/g6, but it will usually come in to play via f7 after ...Nd7 and ...f6.}  
8. h3 {[%eval 0.00][%brsp Bh5]}  Bh5 {[%eval +0.04][%brsp Nc4]}  9. a3 {[%eval 0.00][%brsp a5] This move took me more than 20 minutes, but I felt that it was already time to delve more deeply into the position. In the 2013 world championship Vishy chose the plan with Nf1-g3 without g4, but that was without Qe2 and ...Qe7 included. Now I would just be a tempo down compared that game. Other ways to play were Nc4 or g4 followed by Nf1-g3, but neither of them seemed to give too much of a basis for an interesting battle. Bothering his bishop immediately though, seemed much more interesting. Besides, I won't hide the fact that playing h3 and a3 on consecutive moves in the opening gave me pleasure.}  
(9. Nf1 Nd7 10. Ng3 Bxf3 11. Qxf3 g6 {and White has wasted a tempo.} ) 9... Nd7 {[%eval +0.07][%brsp b4]}  
(9... a5 {Generally this move is desirable strategically, gaining space while preventing b4. However, with the bishop on h5 kingside castling is not attractive with g4 coming and a knight landing on f5, while with the pawn on a5 queenside castling will not be on the cards. Having said that, it was certainly a playable, and probably the most challenging option} 
) 10. b4 {[%eval +0.04][%brsp Bd4]}  Bd6 {[%eval +0.12][%brsp Nc4] Wesley spent almost 20 minutes on this move, which is very much understandable as all three bishop moves were serious options.}  
(10... Bd4 {A very solid option, getting rid the dark squared bishop, which otherwise either risks being harassed on b6, or passive on d6}  
11. Rb1 Bc3 12. O-O Bxd2 {Otherwise the knight escapes}  13. Bxd2 {But I was not too unhappy about this, as the extra space on the queenside feels useful.} 
)  (10... Bb6 {The engines like this move, and I don't have any convincing arguments against it. The worry for Black is that the bishop is likely to be caged in on b6 after a subsequent c4 c5, which an engine probably won't mind. Recent examples from a couple of blitz and rapid games by Nakamura (against MVL and Caruana respectively) in another line of the Berlin, suggest that even top level practice the computer's opinion may prevail.} 
) 11. Nc4 {[%eval +0.18][%brsp c5]}  f6 {[%eval +0.25][%brsp Na5]}  (11... Nf8 {was possible, but I suspect he was worried about}  
12. Na5 {and there is no convenient way to protect b7.} ) 12. Ne3 {[%eval -0.25][%brsp Nf8]A key point of the a3-b4 operation is that the e3-square is freed up for the knight (otherwise the bishop would happily have chopped it off). The knight can later jump to f5 without having to play g4 first.}  
a5 $2 {[%eval +0.21][%brsp Nf5]As on move 9, this is the most challenging, but ceraintly not the most practical option. That's why I'm assigning it a question mark, rather than it's objective value.}  
(12... Nf8 13. Nf5 Qd7 {was very solid; with the knight coming to e6, Black is alright.} 
) 13. Nf5 {[%eval +0.07][%brsp Qf7]}  Qf8 {[%eval +0.50][%brsp bxa5]The consistent follow-up, as he forces me to make a consession on the queenside. However, now his king will be stuck in the center for a while, which is especially unpleasant considering the shorter time-control that was used in Bilbao}  
(13... Qf7 14. Rb1 {Now I don't have to take}  axb4 15. axb4 O-O 16. O-O {and while he has been able to seize the a-file for the moment, the rest of his pieces are rather more clumsily placed than in the note to 12...Nf8}  
(16. Bh6 Qa2 $1) ) 14. bxa5 {[%eval +0.40][%brsp Rxa5]}  Rxa5 {[%eval +0.52][%brsp O-O]}  
15. O-O {[%eval +0.43][%brsp Bxf3]}  Qf7 {[%eval +0.76][%brsp a4]This loss of tempo certainly doesn't look impressive, but at least he frees up the f8-square for the knight or even bishop. Castling however, won't be possible for a long time because of the little trick Bh6, which really is the key to the position.}  
(15... Kd8 {Perhaps he should have tried to evacuate the king to the queenside:} 
 16. a4 (16. Bd2 Ra4) 16... Bb4 { And in both cases it's not so easy to generate a serious initative on the queenside} 
) 16. a4 {[%eval +0.77][%brsp Bxf3]}  Nc5 {[%eval +0.93][%brsp Bd2]}  17. Qe1 {[%eval +0.63][%brsp Ra6]}  
(17. Bd2 {was also good, but this move, freeing up the knight and attacking the rook, just felt too natural not to be played.} 
) 17... b6 {[%eval +1.27][%brsp Nd2]It's becoming harder and harder to defend.}  
(17... Ra8 18. N3h4 { Since the rook will not be attacked after Nc4, the knight goes in the other direction}  
Ne6 (18... Bf8 19. a5 {fixing b7 as a target}  g5 20. Nf3 Bxf3 21. gxf3 {and White is firmly in the driver's seat} 
) 19. Rb1 {and it's hard for Black to avoid losing a pawn, as Nxd6 followed by Nf5 is looming as well.} 
) 18. Nd2 {[%eval +0.51][%brsp Rxa4]}  Rxa4 {[%eval +0.86][%brsp Nc4]}  19. Nc4 {[%eval +0.84][%brsp Be7]The idea. Every move comes with a threat.}  
Bf8 $6 {[%eval +1.53][%brsp Be3]}  (19... Be7 {I suspected that this might better than the text, as it does not block the h8-rook. I was not too worried though, as I saw that I had at least a forced draw}  
20. Be3 Kd7 {The only move, as}  (20... O-O 21. Nxe7+ Qxe7 22. Bxc5 { loses a piece} 
) 21. Nxb6+ (21. Rxa4 Nxa4 22. f4 {This is probably what I would have done. White has a nice initative and is not risking anything, but Black is hanging on for the moment} 
) 21... cxb6 22. Bxc5 Rxa1 23. Qxa1 Bxc5 24. Qa7+ Ke6 25. Nxg7+ Kd6 26. Nf5+ Ke6 
$11) 20. Be3 {[%eval +1.35][%brsp Rg8]}  Kd7 {[%eval +2.51][%assm 2][%brsp Qd2]} 
 (20... Rg8 {This was the only move, which I had seen.}  21. f4 {But still it's impossible to defend, at least in practical play.} 
) 21. Qc3 {[%eval +2.28][%brsp Rxc4]All natural moves, and by now I felt that I was winning. There is very little to be done about the threat of Nxb6 followed by Bxc5, when a white rook will land on a7, winning the queen and the game.}  
Nxe4 {[%eval +4.60][%assm 1][%brsp Nxb6]}  (21... Rxc4 {I thought this was the last chance, but after}  
22. dxc4 Nxe4 23. Qd3+ Nd6 24. g4 Bg6 25. Rfd1 {Black is tied up, while Bxb6 and Ra7 is threatened.} 
) 22. Nxb6+ {[%eval +5.31][%brsp cxb6]Again opening up the seventh rank and leaving the black king completely naked.}  
cxb6 {[%eval +4.99][%brsp dxe4]}  23. dxe4 {[%eval +5.19][%brsp Qc4]}  Qc4 {[%eval +5.35][%brsp Qd2]}  
24. Qd2+ {[%eval +5.35][%brsp Ke8]}  Kc7 {[%eval +5.42][%brsp g4]}  25. g4 {[%eval +5.87][%brsp Bxg4]Finally g4! Much later than expected, but much more powerful.}  
Bg6 {[%eval +7.84][%brsp Rfd1]}  26. Rfd1 {[%eval +9.07][%brsp Ba3]I had no wish to calculate Bxb6 here or on the previous move, as this forces immediate resignation. A fun game, which put me in a great mood (which was immediately ruined by failing to win the next game against Anish, but that's another story) .} 
 1-0

