37. Rxd7 $5 {[%eval +0.81][%brsp Nxd7]It would seem to me, there was logic in keeping the rooks, having more pieces to try and complicate matters, but Magnus believed the black fortress was in no way impregnable.}  
Nxd7 {[%eval +0.85][%brsp Kg3]}  38. Kg3 {[%eval +0.76][%brsp Kf8]}  Kf8 {[%eval +0.85][%brsp f4]}  
39. f4 {[%eval +0.87][%brsp Nf6]}  Nf6 {[%eval +0.97][%brsp Bd3]}  40. Bf3 {[%eval +0.69][%brsp Ke7]}  
Ke7 {[%eval +0.75][%brsp f5]The time-control has been reached, without too much having happened in the game so far. White does of course have the bishop pair, but Black's position is solid, without obvious weaknesses, and White's structure is somewhat shattered, and the knights have strongholds on c6 and f6. It is easy to understand if Nakamura felt reasonably confident he woud draw this position without too much trouble. For a start it's hard to see how White can improve his position, and the next move in many ways looks like worsening it!}  
41. f5 $1 {[%eval +0.79][%brsp gxf5]}  gxf5 {[%eval +0.75][%brsp gxf5]}  42. 
gxf5 {[%eval +0.68][%brsp Ke8] What has White achieved by this exchange? For a start all his pawns are now isolated, as well as the f6-stronghold having become even more solid. Even so there has been considerable progress for White. His king can proceed further up the board, combined with pushing the h-pawn, and one advantage of the bishops over the knights, is that it is the bishop who at any moment can choose to exchange itself. Right now Bxc6 followed by Bg5 is not a threat, as after ...Kf8 Bxf6 gxf6 the pawn ending is drawn as the black king is in time with counterplay on the h-file while the white kings walks to a5, but Black constantly has to be on his guard.}  
Kd7 {[%eval +0.72][%brsp Bg5]}  43. Kf4 {[%eval +0.63][%brsp Kd8]}  Ne8 {[%eval +1.05][%brsp h4]}  
44. Kg5 $1 {[%eval +0.93][%brsp Ke7]As so often, the king becomes a powerful piece in the endgame, and is here excellently placed on g5. After 44...Nf6 45.Bxc6+ followed by 46.Bd4 comes close to forcing a winning pawn ending, but Black seems to draw by a tempo, meaning just 45.h4! being the right response keeping the tension and further improving the white position. And after the tempting 44...f6+ White just plays 45.Kh5! when Bxc6+ followed by Kg6 becomes a decisive threat so Black has no choice, but just has to keep biding his time.}  
Ke7 {[%eval +1.13][%brsp Bf4]}  (44... Nf6 45. h4 $1)  (44... f6+ 45. Kh5 $1) 
45. Bf4 {[%eval +0.99][%brsp Nf6]}  a6 {[%eval +1.35][%brsp Bd2]}  46. h4 {[%eval +1.19][%brsp Kf8]}  
Kf8 {[%eval +1.39][%brsp Bg3]}  47. Bg3 {[%eval +1.42][%brsp Ke7]}  Nf6 {[%eval +1.56][%brsp Bh2]}  
48. Bd6+ {[%eval +1.53][%brsp Ke8]}  Ke8 {[%eval +1.56][%brsp Bh1]}  49. Kf4 {[%eval +1.57][%brsp Nd7]}  
