) 63. Be4 {[%eval +2.88][%brsp Nf2]}  Nf2 {[%eval +2.97][%brsp Bxb7]}  64. Bb1 {[%eval +3.10][%brsp Nb3]}  
Ng4 {[%eval +3.14][%brsp Be4]}  65. Be4 {[%eval +3.03][%brsp Nf2]}  Nf2 {[%eval +3.24][%brsp Bxb7]Black's counterplay makes a lot of sense. In order to proceed, White has to give up the f-pawn further reducing material.}  
66. Bxb7 {[%eval +3.36][%brsp Nd3]}  Nd3 {[%eval +3.34][%brsp Kxf6]The first 40 moves indeed were nothing special, while the next 25 constitute a very instructive ending, where White breaks through a very solid looking fortress. But the brilliancy and tactical complications are here very unusually kept for the last stage of the game, happening after even 6 hours of play.}  
67. Kxf6 $3 {[%eval +3.47][%brsp Nxc5]A stunning piece sacrifice, and the only way to win!}  
(67. Bd6 $2 {[%cbm It looks like]}  Nxf5+ 68. Kxf6 Nxd6 69. Bc6+ $1 Kd8 70. cxd6 
{would lead to an easily winning ending for White, but surprisingly this         
                position is a draw! White simply has no way to get to the   
queenside      with     his              king, without allowing the knight to   
harass the   white    pawns:  }  Nb2 71. Ke6 Nc4 72. a4 a5 73. Kd5 Ne3+ 74. Kc5 
Ng4 {being the principal line. After}  75. Kb6 Ne5 76. Bb5 Nf7 {Black is just in time, as}  
77. d7 Ne5 78. Kxa5 Nxd7 {is a draw, as Black's king reaches the crucial c8-square in the pawn ending.} 
) 67... Nxf4 {[%eval +3.71][%brsp Ke5]}  68. Ke5 $1 {[%eval +3.51][%brsp Nfe2]This is the point. The king attacks both knights, which are forced into a purposeless existence, simply defending each other.}  
Nfe2 {[%eval +3.72][%brsp c6]}  (68... Nde2 {[%cbm After]}  69. Bxa6 {the knights are completely stuck,} 
)  (68... Nxf5 {[%cbm and]}  69. Kxf5 Nd3 70. c6 Kd8 71. Bxa6 {leaves White two pawns up, with no fortress in sight, so Nakamuras plays his best chance.} 
) 69. f6 $2 {[%eval +0.83][%assm 1][%brsp a5] A mistake that could have ruined an otherwise great game and tournament comeback.}  
(69. c6 $1 {would have crowned his efforts, as Black has no other choice than to give up a knight straightaway, as 69...Kd8 70 f6! will lead to the promotion of a white pawn, but after}  
Nxc6+ 70. Bxc6+ Kf8 71. Bd5 $1 { it makes a huge difference compared to the game, that the white pawns are still on a3 as well as on f5. The difference is clearly seen after}  
Nc3 72. Bc4 a5 {as White now doesn't have to defend the a4-pawn and can just simply play}  
73. Kd4 Nd1 74. a4 Ke7 (74... Nb2 {would have been relevant were the white pawn on f6, but here after}  
75. Bb3 Ke7 76. Kc3 Nxa4+ 77. Bxa4 Kf6 {the bishop can defend the pawn, due to it still being on a white square.} 
) 75. Bb3 Nf2 76. Kc5 Kf6 77. Be6 {and again it helps that the pawn is on f5 and White wins by just collecting a5 and then queening the advancing a-pawn while his pawn and bishop securely defend each other.} 
) 69... a5 {[%eval +0.41][%brsp c6]}  70. a4 {[%eval +0.39][%brsp Kf7]Looks winning, as it puts Black in a kind of zugzwang, and, as 70.c6 Nb5 leads nowhere, the logical continuation.}  
Kf7 {[%eval +0.32][%brsp Bd5]}  71. Bd5+ {[%eval +0.31][%brsp Kg6]}  Kf8 $4 {[%eval +5.96][%assm 1][%brsp Ke4]Even so, the drama was not over, as in the commentary room, the computers indicated that White had only one way to exploit the favour, and Magnus took almost all of his remaining 2 minutes on the clock, before calmly retreating his king.}  
(71... Kg6 $1 {would have seen Black just escape, as after}  72. c6 Nxc6+ 73. 
Bxc6 Kf7 74. Bd5+ Kf8 {it makes a deciding difference that the white pawns are now on a4 and f6, and thus much easier to attack. After}  
75. Bc4 Nc3 76. Bb3 {Black just bides his time with}  Ke8 {and}  77. Kd4 Ne2+ {almost by magic draws. The crucial point is}  
78. Kc4 (78. Kc5 {[%cbm and as well]}  Nc3 $1 {threatening to take on a4, and after the bishop takes back, then ...Kf7 clearing off the last pawn. It still feels very counter-intuitive to me, that this, as well as the ending analysed above both are drawn for Black, but the computer does verify that there is no way, White can exploit his seemingly big superiority.} 
) 78... Nc1 $1 {attacking the bishop, and then having ...Kf7 next winning the f6-pawn,} 
) 72. Ke4 $1 {[%eval +6.68][%brsp Ke8]The point is that is the king reaches e3, the knihts are doomed, as the king attacks both, and Bc4 next will just pick one up for free, so Nakamura tries:}  
Nc2 {[%eval +8.98][%brsp c6]}  73. c6 {[%eval +34.77][%brsp Nc3]}  Nc3+ {[%eval +34.77][%brsp Ke5]}  
