4. Nc3 {[%eval +0.28][%brsp Bb4]}  dxc4 {[%eval +0.34][%brsp e4]Duda stays loyal to his tournament preparation, the Vienna had netted him 2 effortless draws in the tournament already.}  
5. e4 {[%eval +0.31][%brsp Bb4]}  Bb4 {[%eval +0.35][%brsp Bg5]}  6. Bxc4 $5 {[%eval +0.31][%brsp Nxe4]In the first round Vidit played the traditional 6 Bg5 whereafter Duda surprised with 6...b5!? 7.a4 c5. Magnus follows Duda's game with Mamedyarov, where White played the now popular pure pawn sacrifice, aiming for quick development.}  
Nxe4 {[%eval +0.27][%brsp O-O]}  7. O-O {[%eval +0.34][%brsp Nf6]}  Nf6 $5 {[%eval +0.23][%brsp Bg5]}  
(7... Bxc3 8. bxc3 Nxc3 {gives Black even 2 extra pawns, but the white initiative becomes overwhelming. Danish IM Steffen Pedersen and Swedish GM Tiger Hillarp Pedersen showed me this idea way before it became popular, and I managed to win a blitz game agasnt Van Wely in the German Team Championship before forgetting about it, and being completely lost against Pedersen himself in an blindfold event in Denmark 2007. Won by Magnus himself, as it was organised in order to give him training for his first appearance in the Melody Amber Tournament.}  
9. Qb3 Nd5 10. Ba3 Nc6 11. Rfe1 Nce7 12. Bxe7 Nxe7 13. d5 exd5 14. Bxd5 O-O 15. 
Bxf7+ $1 Rxf7 16. Rad1 Qf8 17. Ng5 {is the basic tactical point, and my game against Pedersen from 2007.} 
) 8. Qa4+ {[%eval +0.26][%brsp Nc6]}  Nc6 {[%eval +0.26][%brsp Ne5]}  9. Ne5 {[%eval +0.26][%brsp Rb8]}  
Rb8 {[%eval +0.30][%brsp Rd1]Obviously the line has evolved, and Black found more sensible solutions than the early greedy attempts. 9...Rb8 is Caruana's choice against Aronian from the Norway Chess tournament 2018, but obviously part of the American's preparation for the candidates tournament. Funnily it was already played by Magnus' second Laurent Fressinet in 2010, whose loss had nothing to do with his position after the opening.}  
10. Rd1 $5 {[%eval +0.30][%brsp O-O]Aronian took on c6, and Halkias played 10.d5. Magnus plays a less direct approach, but blitzed out the following moves obviously still in his preparation. Duda knew the risks. Playing Caruana's favourite line, which Magnus obviously must have prepared, with hindsight might seem like a bad idea, but the young Pole showed numerous times in Wijk that he is not afraid of a challenge.}  
O-O {[%eval +0.32][%brsp Nxc6]}  11. Nxc6 {[%eval +0.45][%brsp bxc6]}  bxc6 {[%eval +0.49][%brsp Qxa7]}  
12. Qxa7 {[%eval +0.41][%brsp Rb6]Quite a typical scenario in modern chess: White threatened a blitz attack by sacrificing a pawn, Black choose a solid continuation, and now suddenly White changes gears, now playing for a better pawn structure and a potentially passed a-pawn.}  
Bd7 {[%eval +0.35][%brsp Qa4]Threatening a "perpetual" against White's queen with ... Ra8/...Rb8.}  
13. Qa4 {[%eval +0.29][%brsp e5]}  c5 {[%eval +0.41][%brsp Qc2]}  14. Qc2 {[%eval +0.32][%brsp Bc6]This is why White included 10.Rd1 0-0, he needed the rook to protect the d4-pawn.}  
cxd4 {[%eval +0.48][%brsp Rxd4]}  15. Rxd4 {[%eval +0.44][%brsp h6]}  Bc5 $6 {[%eval +0.53][%brsp Rd1]Magnus mentioned in a video interview after the tournament that he recalled asking some of his second trying to sell him on this concept and he asked why can black not just go 15...Bc5 followed by 16...h6? Somebody switched on the engine, and said 17.Bxh6 and 18.Qd2 is +1. But no one told me what this +1 consisted of.}  
16. Rh4 $5 {[%eval +0.34][%brsp Re8]It was notheworthy that the Norwegian supercomputer "sesse" preferred 16.Rd1, aiming for more positional methods.}  
h6 $2 {[%eval +0.79][%brsp Bxh6]Played quickly, but walking straight into preparation. Or sort of preparation at least:}  
17. Bxh6 $1 {[%eval +0.76][%brsp gxh6]}  gxh6 {[%eval +0.80][%brsp Qd2]}  18. 
Qd2 {[%eval +0.78][%brsp Nd5]Blacks problem is that h6 cannot be protected, and losing it basically leads to mate on h8.}  
Nh7 $1 {[%eval +0.76][%brsp Qxh6]Or 18...Ne8 leading to the same thing.}  (18... 
