1. b3 {[%eval -0.30][%brsp e5]The game rarely truly begins at the first move, but with Jobava it is no great surprise. The Georgian player is exceptionally gifted, but not your standard top grandmaster. In his chess philosophy opening knowledge does not deserve any prominent spot.}  
g6 {[%eval +0.14][%brsp Bb2]}  2. Bb2 {[%eval +0.11][%brsp Nf6]}  Nf6 {[%eval +0.19][%brsp Nf3]}  
3. Nc3 {[%eval -0.30][%brsp d5]}  Bg7 {[%eval +0.10][%brsp Ne4]}  4. d4 {[%eval -0.30][%brsp c5]}  
c5 {[%eval -0.30][%brsp e3]Carlsen is a clear favourite in the game and the world champion actively looks for early conflict.}  
(4... d5 5. Qd2 a5 6. f3 h5 7. e4 dxe4 8. fxe4 a4 9. bxa4 Bh6 10. Qd3 b6 11. Nf3 
Ba6 12. Qd1 Bxf1 13. Rxf1 Nc6 14. d5 Na5 15. Qd4 c5 16. Qd3 O-O 17. h3 e6 18. d6 
Nd7 19. g4 h4 20. e5 $14 {1-0 (48) Shengelia,D (2556) -Schreiner,P (2460) Austria 2013} 
) 5. e3 {[%eval -0.35][%brsp cxd4]}  cxd4 {[%eval -0.37][%brsp Qxd4]}  6. exd4 {[%eval -0.40][%brsp d5]}  
d5 {[%eval -0.35][%brsp Nf3]The choice of the central structure was done in a hope, that the development of the bishop to b2 will turn out to have been slightly premature.}  
(6... O-O 7. Bd3 Nc6 8. Nge2 d6 9. O-O Bg4 10. f3 Bd7 11. Ne4 Nd5 12. Qd2 f5 13. 
Bc4 e6 14. Nxd6 Qh4 15. Nxb7 Bh6 16. f4 g5 17. g3 Qh5 18. Nc5 $18 {0-1 (37) Bancod,R (2171)-Gallegos,P  (2097) ICC INT 2009} 
) 7. Qd2 {[%eval -0.52][%brsp O-O]}  Nc6 {[%eval -0.34][%brsp Bb5]}  8. f3 {[%eval -0.96][%brsp h5]}  
h5 {[%eval -0.77][%brsp g3]In retrospect Black might have been somewhat unhappy about the missed chance to limit the scope of the light-squared bishop of White.}  
(8... a6 $5 9. O-O-O h5 $15) 9. Bb5 {[%eval -0.98][%brsp Qc7]}  Qd6 {[%eval -0.80][%brsp Nge2]}  
