Nxa4 {[%eval 0.00]}  20. Qc2 {[%eval -0.25]}  Bd7 {[%eval +0.12]}  21. b3 {[%eval +0.08]Commenting on every move of a rapid game is not fair, the computer will of course suggest a number of improvements. White's play is logical, trying to force Black's knight to retreat to c5 when after taking it White keeps a promising position. But from here Magnus just plays the following complications perfectly:}  
f4 $1 {[%eval +0.11]}  22. bxa4 {[%eval -0.08]}  fxe3 {[%eval -0.10]}  23. axb5 {[%eval -0.16]}  
Rxa1 {[%eval -0.19]}  24. Rxa1 {[%eval -0.19]}  Bg5 $1 {[%eval -0.18]White's passed b-pawn obviously is a huge asset, so Black has to hurry. This threatens on f2, and if White plays 25 fxe3 then 25...Bxe3+ followed by 26...Rf2 and 27...Bb6 gives Black a dominant position. Ian replies with the logical and critical}  
25. b6 {[%eval -0.23]}  exf2+ {[%eval -0.26]}  26. Kh1 {[%eval -0.40]}  Bc8 {[%eval -0.35]}  
27. Rf1 {[%eval -0.29]}  Qf7 {[%eval -0.24]}  28. Qe2 {[%eval -0.13]At first sight there are reasons for White to be optimistic. The f2-pawn is securely blockaded, and while White has no imminent counter threats, he can improve the situation with h4 and Kh2 next, safeguarding his king. By logic, Black should act immediately, but how?}  
e4 $1 {[%eval 0.00]The idea is 29 Qxe4 Ba6, attacking the rook on f1, and thus breaking the bloackade. Nepoom spots an ingenious counter-resource:}  
29. h4 $5 {[%eval -2.21]The point is that after 29...Bh6 30.Bxe4 Bh3 White now has 31.Ne7+!! as the black bishop no longer defends e7. 31...Qxe7 loses to 32 Bxh7+ and after 31...Kh8 32 Bg2 is now possible, the point being that 32...Bxg2 33 Kxg2 Qxd5+no longer is possible as the knight from e7 protects the d5-pawn!}  
e3 $3 {[%eval -2.35]An amazing counter! Black just calmly gives up a piece, what matters is to keep his connected passed pawns and advance them.}  
(29... Bh6 30. Bxe4 Bh3 31. Ne7+ $3 {[%csl Ge7,Yh6]}  Kh8 (31... Qxe7 32. Bxh7+) 
32. Bg2 Bxg2+ 33. Kxg2 {[%cal Yf7d5][%csl Ge7]} ) 30. hxg5 {[%eval -2.30]}  Re8 
$1 {[%eval -2.42]Protects e3 and calmly prepares ...Qg6xg5 followed by a deadly check in the h-file.}  
31. Kh2 $1 {[%eval -2.21]With the idea of 31... Qg6 32 Bh3!, White again forces Black to show his attacking ceativity. And once again he does not fear further material losses to advance his pawns:}  
Bg4 $3 {[%eval -2.40]}  32. Qxg4 {[%eval -2.29]}  e2 {[%eval -2.45]Like Labourdonnais! Magnus' idea is not just to win a rook, but to actually queen the pawns! Black's threat is 33...e1Q, however, with the detail that 33.Nd8!? is met by 33...exf1N+!  White has no reasonable defence, so Nepo tries fighthing it with two pieces for the queen, but loses in the long run of course.}  
