1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 c6 6. e3 Bf5 7. Qf3 Bg6 8. Bxf6 
Qxf6 9. Qxf6 gxf6 {Kramnik had played this strategically slightly risky variation before. Since the Berlin Ruy Lopez is his main weapon against 1.e4, it is no great wonder that against 1.d4 he does not have doubled pawns either. But on the other hand the pawns are less compact here and secondly accepting weaknesses against a subtle positional player like Carlsen looks a bit like waving a red flag in the front of a bull.}  
10. Nf3 Nd7 11. Nh4 Be7 {This is the main line, preparing to meet f2-f4 with ...f7-f5.}  
(11... Nb6 12. f4 f5 13. Bd3 Nc4 14. Bxc4 dxc4 15. a4 $14 {1/2-1/2 (31) Williams,S (2439)-Hawkins,J  (2563) London 2015} 
)  (11... O-O-O $6 12. f4 h5 13. Kf2 Re8 14. f5 Bh6 15. fxg6 Bxe3+ 16. Kf3 Bxd4 
17. g7 Rhg8 18. Nf5 $18 {Bareev,E (2636)-Gorman,D (2289) Philadelphia 2009 (1-0, 40)} 
) 12. Ne2 $5 $146 {An early novelty connected with a deep regrouping plan. Actually the last move is logical since it prepares to invade f5 before Black carries out the standard knight manoeuvre to d6.}  
(12. g3 {[%cbm  Kramnik's previous games went]}  Nb6 13. O-O-O (13. Kd2 Nc8 14. 
Bd3 Nd6 15. b3 O-O-O 16. f3 Rhe8 17. Rac1 Kb8 18. Ne2 Nc8 19. Nf4 Bb4+ 20. Ke2 
Nd6 21. g4 Re7 $11 {Ivanisevic,I (2653)-Kramnik,V (2777) Berlin 2015 (0-1, 53)} 
) 13... Nc8 14. Bd3 Nd6 15. Kc2 Kd7 16. f3 Bxd3+ 17. Kxd3 f5 18. Ng2 h5 19. h4 
Rag8 20. Rh3 Ne8 21. Ke2 Bd6 22. Kf2 Nf6 23. Ne2 a5 $11 {Ding,L (2730)-Kramnik,V (2760) Moscow 2014 (1/2-1/2, 41)} 
) 12... Nb6 {Since the knight will not reach d6 in time, this move is now connected woth a queenside plan which will prove illusory, though.}  
(12... f5 13. g3 Nb6 14. Nf4 Nc8 15. f3 Nd6 16. Bd3 $14 {[%cal Ge1f2,Gh1c1,Gb2b4,Ga2a4] /\ Kf2, b4} 
)  (12... Bb4+ {[%cbm I have tried making work the break ...c6-c5 by transferring the knight to e6, but this does not seem to be entirely satisfactory either:]}  
13. Kd1 O-O-O 14. Ng3 Nf8 15. Ngf5 Ne6 16. Bd3 Ba5 17. Ke2 (17. Kc2 c5 {[%cal Gg6c2]} 
) 17... Rhe8 18. g3 Bb6 19. Rad1 Kb8 {[%cal Ge6d4]}  (19... Bh5+ 20. f3 Nxd4+ 
21. Nxd4 Bxd4 22. Bf5+ $1 $18) 20. Kf1 (20. b4 Bh5+ 21. f3 Nxd4+) 20... c5 21. 
dxc5 Nxc5 (21... Bxc5 22. Kg2 d4 23. e4 $14) 22. Kg2 (22. Bc2 Ne6 23. Kg2 d4 24. 
e4 $6 Nc5 $36) 22... Nxd3 23. Rxd3 Re5 24. g4 (24. Nxg6 hxg6 25. Nd4 Re4) 24... 
h5 25. h3 $14) 13. Ng3 $14 Bb4+ (13... Nc8 14. f4 $16 h6 15. f5 {leaves the light-squared bishop out of play for the rest of the game.} 
) 14. Kd1 Na4 15. Ngf5 $1 {During the game I was not sure about the meaning of this regrouping. At first sight, the knights are clumsily placed, being neutralised by one bishop. Everything became clear a few moves later.}  
Kd7 (15... Nxb2+ 16. Kc2 Nc4 17. Bxc4 dxc4 18. Rhb1 $16) 16. Rb1 Ke6 $6 {As it turns out in the game, the king is exposed on e6.}  
(16... Rhc8 $5 17. Bd3 Bf8 (17... Bd6 18. Ke2 a6 19. g4 c5 20. Nxd6 Kxd6 21. 
Nf5+ $16) 18. Ke2 a6 19. g4 c5 20. Ng2 Bd6 21. h4 (21. Nxd6 Kxd6 22. Nh4 {would practically lose a tempo over the previous line.}  
cxd4 23. exd4 Rc6) 21... h5 22. f3 Rc6 23. gxh5 Bxh5 24. Nxd6 Kxd6 25. Nf4 Rh8 
26. Bc2 Nb6 27. dxc5+ Rxc5 28. Bb3 $14) 17. Bd3 Rhc8 18. Ke2 Bf8 19. g4 c5 $6 {This only creates new weaknesses and no counterplay at all.}  
(19... Kd7 $5) 20. Ng2 $3 $16 {[%calGh2h4,Gg2f4] The core of Carlsen's whole opening plan. Suddenly Black loses stability in several sectors of the board.}  
cxd4 21. exd4 Bd6 {[%csl Gf4]}  22. h4 h5 $2 {This loses a pawn leading to a hopeless position.}  
(22... Rh8 $8 {[%cbm But it is virtually impossible to decide upon a passive move like][%cslGh7] defending the h7-pawn.}  
23. h5 Bxf5 24. Bxf5+ Ke7 25. Ne3 Nb6 26. Kf3 h6 27. Rhe1 Kd8 28. Rbc1 $16 {Black's position looks awful, but White still needs to prove he has a winning plan.} 
) 23. Ng7+ Ke7 24. gxh5 Bxd3+ 25. Kxd3 $18 { The control over the f5-square and the threatening h-pawns make Black's position hopeless.}  
Kd7 26. Ne3 Nb6 27. Ng4 $5 {Even though one of the knights belongs to f5, Carlsen's "rodeos" are impressive at least optically.}  
Rh8 28. Rhe1 {Playing cat and mouse a bit.}  (28. Nxf6+ {[%cbm There was nothing wrong with]}  
Ke7 29. Ng4) 28... Be7 29. Nf5 Bd8 30. h6 Rc8 31. b3 {[%csl Gb6]}  Rc6 32. Nge3 
Bc7 33. Rbc1 Rxc1 34. Rxc1 Bf4 35. Rc5 Ke6 36. Ng7+ Kd6 37. Ng4 Nd7 38. Rc2 f5 {A desperate move, which loses a pawn for nothing.}  
(38... a6 39. Re2 {[%calGe2e8]} )  (38... Ke7 39. Re2+ Kd8 40. Re8+ $5 (40. Nf5 
$18) 40... Rxe8 41. Nxe8 Bxh6 42. Nexf6 Nxf6 43. Nxf6 $18 {[%csl Gd5]} ) 39. 
Nxf5+ Ke6 40. Ng7+ {[%cslGf5,Gf6] As we see, 38...f5 did not clear space for the black king at all.}  
Kd6 41. Re2 Kc6 42. Re8 Rxe8 (42... Rh7 43. Nf5 {[%csl Gh7]} ) 43. Nxe8 Nf8 44. 
Ne5+ Bxe5 45. dxe5 Kd7 46. Nf6+ Ke6 47. h5 Kxe5 48. Nd7+ Nxd7 49. h7 Nc5+ 50. 
Ke2 { A fantastic game by Carlsen and the ugliest defeat of Kramnik I can remember.} 
 1-0

