1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 {It is kind of funny that Carlsen has played only 4 classical games in the Scotch Game since he became a grandmaster. Probably another reason for Wesley So to try it against the reigning World Champion.}  
Bb4+ $5 {An off-beat variation, forcing White to play c3, and removing that ideal square for the b1-knight. Magnus himself said he wasn't expecting the Scotch and just thought about playing some variation to try and develop his pieces.}  
(4... Nf6 {is the most popular response, with well-known variations after the most ambitious 5.Nxc6 followed by 6.e5, or the simpler 5. Nc3.} 
)  (4... Bc5 {when White has options between the old moves Nxc6 and Be3, or the more fashionable Nb3 followed by Nc3, Qe2, Be3 or Bg5 and possibly castling queenside.} 
) 5. c3 Be7 $6 {should be inferior for Black. We arrive in a Philidor-type position, where White has sound pieces and a space advantage.}  
(5... Bc5 $5 {is also possible, now in the Nxc6 and Nb3 variations, the b1-knight would like to go to c3.}  
6. Be3 Bb6 {and here White would be happy to play Nc3-Nd5 as well, justifying the ...Bb4+ and ...Bc5 manoeuvre.}  
(6... Qf6 $2 {now this wouldn't make any sense, since the pawn is already on c3  (this is played in the line after 4...Bc5 where White still has the pawn on c2) } 
) 7. Nf5 $1 g6 8. Bxb6 axb6 9. Ne3 Nf6 10. f3 Qe7 (10... O-O 11. c4 {and White tries to keep a positional advantage. Black has an interesting piece sacrifice with ...d5 now:}  
d5 12. cxd5 Nxe4 13. fxe4 Qh4+ 14. Kd2 Qxe4 15. Bd3 Qd4 16. Qb3 Re8 17. Re1 Ne5 
18. Na3 Nxd3 $2 (18... Bd7 $5 $40) 19. Qxd3 $6 (19. Nac2 $1 $18) 19... Qxb2+ 20. 
Nac2 Bd7 (20... Qe5 $1 $132) 21. Qc3 $16 {1-0 (30) Danin,A (2581)-Zeller,F (2402) Wunsiedel 2016} 
) 11. Be2 $5 (11. c4 Qb4+ 12. Qd2 Qxd2+ 13. Kxd2 {with a pleasant endgame}  d6 
14. Nc3 Kd8 15. Be2 Re8 16. Rac1 $14 {1-0 (40) Yu,Y (2662)-Salem,A (2570) Kocaeli 2013} 
) 11... Qc5 12. Qd2 h5 $6 13. Na3 Qe7 $2 14. Nd5 Nxd5 15. exd5 Ne5 16. d6 $1 
Qxd6 17. Qxd6 cxd6 18. Nb5 Ke7 19. a4 $18 {1-0 (47) Duda,J (2693)-Degraeve,J (2592) Chartres 2017} 
) 6. Nxc6 bxc6 7. Bd3 (7. Bc4 $5 Nf6 8. e5 $36) 7... d6 8. O-O Nf6 9. Re1 O-O 
10. Nd2 {I like White's position here, the knight will be well placed on f3, Black has to be watching for moves like e5.}  
Re8 11. Nf3 Nd7 {A natural reaction - the knight stands well on c5 in these kind of structures, but this will be the start of his problems as well.}  
12. Bf4 Nc5 13. Bc2 Bg4 $6 (13... Ne6 $5 14. Be3 c5 {followed by ...Rb8 and ...a5}  
15. b4 $5 {trying to avoid that plan}  cxb4 16. cxb4 $14) 14. h3 Bh5 15. Be3 $1 {Threatening to destroy Black's structure; this is the key move to look for an advantage. Carlsen said in the post-mortem that his position started to deteriorate now, after this and 16. Ba4!, that he missed.}  
Nd7 (15... Qb8 16. Bxc5 dxc5 17. Rb1 $16 {has to be ugly for Black.} ) 16. Ba4 
$1 c5 (16... Ne5 $2 17. g4 $1 Nxf3+ 18. Qxf3 Bg6 19. Bxc6 $18 {is simply lost for Black.} 
) 17. g4 $5 (17. e5 $1 {was a very interesting pawn sacrifice:}  Bxf3 (17... 
Nxe5 18. g4 $1 Bg6 19. Nxe5 dxe5 20. Bxe8 $18)  (17... Rb8 18. b3 Bxf3 19. Qxf3 
Nxe5 20. Qe4 Rf8 21. Rad1 $44) 18. Qxf3 Nxe5 19. Qe4 {hitting the e8-rook}  Rf8 
20. Rad1 {threatening to take on c5 }  Qc8 21. Bxc5 (21. b4 $5 cxb4 22. cxb4 $44 
{trying to maintain the pressure, without exchanging anything.} ) 21... dxc5 22. 
Qxe5 Bd6 23. Qe4 $16 {with good prospects.} ) 17... Bg6 18. e5 (18. Bc6 $1 Rb8 
19. b4 {was a very strong hidden idea}  h5 $1 {with a double-edged position, that should be safe enough for White }  
(19... cxb4 $2 20. Qa4 $1 {winning the e8 exchange by force, because now b6 is under control}  
(20. Bxa7 $6 Ra8 $1 21. Bxa8 $2 Qxa8 22. Be3 Bxe4 $19 { is horrible for White} ) 
20... Nb6 21. Bxb6 axb6 22. Bxe8 Qxe8 23. Qxe8+ Rxe8 24. cxb4 $18 {should be simply winning} 
) 20. bxc5 hxg4 21. hxg4 $16) 18... Rb8 19. Bf4 $2 {Now many simplifications take place, Carlsen equalises and gets the initiative suddenly.}  
(19. b3 $1 $16 {was simple and strong, Black still has to solve many problems.} 
) 19... Rxb2 $1 20. exd6 $2 (20. Bxd7 $1 {was the best chance to equalise}  Qxd7 
21. exd6 cxd6 22. Ne5 Qc7 (22... Qc8 23. Nxg6 hxg6 24. Bxd6 $11) 23. Nc4 $11) 
20... Bxd6 $1 21. Rxe8+ Qxe8 22. Bxd6 cxd6 23. Qxd6 (23. Nd2 {the computer suggests this very sad idea to survive, a hard decision to make after giving away a pawn. On the other hand, there is a Nc4 threat}  
d5 24. Nb3 Qe7 25. Bxd7 Qxd7 26. Nxc5 Qd6 27. Qd4 h5 $36) 23... Qe2 $1 {Maybe So missed this move.}  
24. Qg3 Nf8 $1 $17 {White has many problems now, with the weak king, weak pawns and having to control Black's initiative.}  
25. Re1 Rb1 $1 26. Rxb1 Bxb1 {At least the a2-pawn will be lost, White is in deep trouble.}  
27. Bc6 (27. a3 Be4 $1 {with mating ideas as well}  28. Nh2 Qe1+ 29. Nf1 Qb1 $1 {threatening the unstoppable ...Bd3}  
30. f4 Bd3 31. Qf3 c4 $19 { Black will take on c3 at the proper time and win the game.} 
) 27... Bxa2 $19 28. Qd6 Qc4 $1 29. Ne5 Qxc3 {With no counterplay left, and two pawns down, White had to resign.} 
 0-1

