1. e4 {The first time I've ever tried 1.e4 against Mickey in classical chess}  
e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 {But alas, the free day before the game was not spent refuting his Ruy/Berlin repertoire}  
Bc5 4. O-O d6 {Avoiding the Max Power (Max Lange) attack after ...Nf6 d4.}  5. 
c3 Nf6 6. d3 {I remembered that Saric got an advantage with d4 Bb6 dxe5 against Aronian in last year's Tata, but I assumed Mickey had that covered.}  
h6 {Avoiding Bg5. Against the Italian such liberties can often be taken. Besides he stays flexible on the queenside, deciding whether to play ...a6 or ...a5 only after I've shown some of my cards. }  
7. a4 {I spent more than 25 minutes combined on this and the next move, time that I would later curse myself for having wasted. Part of the time was spent pondering whether the extra h6-move could somehow be used to my advantage after d4 Bb6 (I cannot imagine why I thought it maybe could). But mostly I was trying to figure how I could favourably expand on the queenside.}  
(7. b4 Bb6 8. Nbd2 {was one of the lines I considered, as if I get time for Bb3 and Nc4 I will have a little something.}  
Ne7 {However looked fine to me, as Black will be in time to meet Nc4 with ...Bc7.} 
)  (7. Nbd2 a6 {also gives Black a normal Italian.}  (7... Ne7 8. d4 {is probably an achievement for White} 
) ) 7... a5 { Probably the right decision. If ...a6 (one of the two naturally had to be played to avoid me trapping his bishop) then I would follow the same plan, with a little more space than in the game.}  
8. Na3 {Once again I spent time considering d4, but finally decided to just play normally.}  
O-O {I actually thought he might consider just chopping off my knight, as my rook would be temporarily misplaced, and I was not sure my dark-squared bishop would have such a bright future. The bishop pair will soon be gone after ...Be6.}  
9. Nc2 Re8 {I was quite happy to see this move, as I will be able to pin his rook after ...Be6 Bb5.}  
(9... Be6 10. Bxe6 fxe6 {was naturally quite possible, but White could claim to be slightly better after Be3 Bxe3 Nxe3, probably without justification.} 
) 10. Re1 {As he cannot go Be6 Bxe6 Rxe6 due to d4-d5, it makes sense to make a waiting move before going Be3.}  
Ba7 11. Be3 Be6 12. Bb5 Bd7 { Now I felt that I just needed to make another waiting move, and he would have to make a slight concession.}  
13. Bxa7 {After a frustrating half an hour of thinking I could not find anything better.}  
(13. h3 {is the move that suggests itself. But I did not want to weaken f4}  Ne7 
14. Bxa7 Rxa7 15. d4 Ng6 {It seemed to me that I would not be able to exploit the undefended position of his rook on a7 after dxe5, and otherwise Black is solid, and might find some counterplay with ...Nf4 later.} 
)  (13. Rc1)  (13. g3)  (13. Nd2)  (13. Qd2)  (13. Qe2 {were other more sophisticated means I considered in the fight for a tempo, but none of them came close to convincing me.} 
) 13... Nxa7 14. Bc4 Be6 { Very logical. He could also go ...Nc8 immediately, but then I might go hide the bishop:}  
(14... Nc8 15. Ne3 Nb6 16. Bb3 Be6 17. Bc2 {with d4 coming, although it is naturally not much.} 
) 15. Bxe6 Rxe6 16. Ne3 Nc8 {The best route for the knight.}  (16... Nc6 17. Qb3 
{with Nd5 looming, is a little uncomfortable.} ) 17. Nf5 {The white pieces are a little better placed, but with such a symmetrical structure my slight advantage will naturally disappear. That's why I took the chance to force matters a little.}  
Ne7 18. d4 {The idea of my previous move.}  (18. N3h4 {is pointless, as without the dark-squared bishops I cannot make use of the knight on f5.} 
) 18... exd4 19. N3xd4 Re5 $2 { A mistake, though neither of us really understood that during the game.}  
(19... Rxe4 20. Rxe4 Nxe4 21. Qg4 {At first seemed to me quite promising for White, as I somehow only considered Nxf5 here}  
Ng5 $1 {I discovered that this was possible only after I played 17.Nf5}  (21... 
Nxf5 22. Nxf5 Ng5 23. Re1 $1 { is what I liked for White; h4 is now a serious threat and after}  
Qf6 24. Ne7+ Kh8 25. Nd5 Qd8 26. Qf5 $1 Qc8 27. Qxc8+ Rxc8 28. Re7 {White's initiative persists without queens} 
) 22. Re1 (22. h4 Nxf5 23. Nxf5 Ne6 24. Nxh6+ Kf8 25. Nf5 Qf6 {is objectively the best, with equality} 
) 22... Ng6 $1 23. h4 Nh7 { and there is nothing concrete while Black remains a pawn up.} 
) 20. Ng3 Ng6 { It's easy to understand why Mickey did not feel the need to play concretely, Black seems very solid, while my space advantage (e4 vs d6) does not feel very significant with two pairs of minor pieces exchanged. Besides it appears that the space advantage could disappear at any moment after a timely d6-d5. His last move is quite necessary to avoid f4, as rolling with a Lasker rook on c5 understandably seemed a bit too funky.}  
21. Qc2 {I spent seven minutes on this move, partly hesitating between going c4 immediately and bringing my rook into play first, and partly cursing myself having wasted so much time earlier in the game and now wasting more precious time (I had about half an hour left before this move) on a decision that did not seem particularly significant.}  
(21. c4 {I finally decided against it because of}  c6 {with ...Qb6 coming, even after}  
22. Ndf5 Qb6 {and ...Rd8 follows after either capture.} ) 21... c6 { Not a great move, but very logical. Engines start suggesting moves like Qb8-a7, which is not very intuitive while White has a square on b5.}  
22. Rad1 {Now I was very surprised and happy to discover that I was actually better! It's hard to play ...d5 under favourable circumstances and my plan in the game is surprisingly powerful.}  
Qc7 (22... d5 {does not quite work}  23. Nf3 Re8 24. exd5 Rxe1+ 25. Nxe1 cxd5 
(25... Nxd5 26. c4) 26. Nf3 {is comfortably better for White. Normally it would not be much, but the inclusion of a4 a5 helps me enormously, as there will be a square on b5 and additional targets.} 
) 23. Ndf5 Rd8 (23... d5 24. exd5 Nxd5 (24... cxd5 {is better, but again by no means equal } 
) 25. Nh5 {is not difficult, but it was somehow aesthetically pleasing during the game. g7 cannot be defended.}  
Rae8 26. Rf1 (26. Rxe5 Qxe5 27. Qb1 $1 { is also possible}  (27. g3 Qe2) ) 26... 
Re2 27. Qb1 {and Black's temporary activity is extinguished, while g7 falls.} ) 
24. Qd2 {Going c4 was also possible, with a nice grip on the position, but I was excited about the prospect of attacking d6 and threatening f4 with one move.}  
Kh7 {Played after 18 minutes of thinking, a sure sign that he realised that his position was getting really unpleasant.}  
(24... Qb6 25. Kh1 {does not seem to change much.}  (25. Nxd6 Re6 {is much too hasty} 
) ) 25. f4 Qb6+ {Normally one would like to save such a check for later, but here it is absolutely forced.}  
(25... Ree8 26. Nxd6 Qb6+ 27. Qf2 {Now I can safely block the check, since the rook on e8 is hanging.} 
) 26. Kh1 (26. Qf2 {Now f4 is simply hanging:}  Qxf2+ 27. Kxf2 Nxf4) 26... Ree8 
(26... Re6 27. e5 {is absolutely terrible for Black, as long as I avoid a nasty little trick}  
Ng4 28. Rf1 $1 {I was going to play this on general grounds, as one should not take the rook off the last rank for no reason!}  
(28. Re2 $4 Nxf4 $1 29. Qxf4 dxe5 {This is the point; because of the last rank weakness the tables are turned.} 
) ) 27. h3 {By far the best move, but again I invested a large part of the time I had left.}  
(27. Nxd6 Ng4 28. Rf1 Rxd6 (28... Ne3 29. Qf2 $1) 29. Qxd6 Nf2+ 30. Rxf2 Qxf2 {and I am only temporarily a pawn up as both f4 and b2 are hanging.} 
) 27... Rd7 {This came as a major surprise to me. Black cannot defend d6 as both ...d5 and ...Re6 run into e5, but while maybe not objectively better,}  
(27... h5 {is what I spent all my time calculating before I played h3 and would have been a very interesting try. I investigated several options:}  
28. Qe2 $1 {The surprising move is the one I liked the most during the game}  
(28. Qc2 {or Qd3 were other moves that I briefly touched on, with the idea of answering}  
h4 {with}  29. Nxh4 Nxh4 30. e5+ {But if he simply defends the pawn with ...Qc7 or ...Qc5 instead, I was not convinced about my advantage} 
)  (28. Nxd6 {This is the simplest. After the forced}  h4 29. Ngf5 Nxe4 {I saw}  
30. Nxe4 $1 Rxe4 $1 {but I could not evaluate}  (30... Rxd2 31. Ng5+ {mates:}  
Kg8 32. Rxe8+ Nf8 33. Ne7+ Kh8 34. Rxf8#) 31. Rxe4 Rxd2 32. Rxd2 {properly. With more time I would surely have realised that all the entry points for his queen are covered and my threat of Nd6 is very strong, but with little time and a possibly unsafe king, I'm always a bit wary of playing against the queen.} 
) 28... h4 (28... Nxf4 { Normally one would stop calculating here, but since my queen is now no longer pinned on the d-file}  
29. Qf3 {followed by Nxd6 is now very strong} ) 29. Nh5 Nxe4 30. Qf3 $1 {I saw this idea, but I was still apprehensive, as I could not really believe that it would work. But it does!}  
Nf2+ (30... Qc5 {This is a much better defence, but it does not solve his problems}  
31. Nhxg7 Ng3+ 32. Nxg3 Rxe1+ 33. Rxe1 hxg3 34. Nh5 Qf2 35. Qxg3 Qxg3 36. Nxg3 {and since ...Nxf4 is met by Rf1 White has good winning chances} 
) 31. Kh2 Nxd1 32. Nf6+ $1 gxf6 33. Qh5+ Kg8 34. Qh6 {and mates.} ) 28. Nxd6 
Rxd6 {The only chance:}  (28... Red8 29. e5 Ne8 30. Ngf5 {is just a pawn.} ) 29. 
Qxd6 Qf2 {Since all my attention had been focused on 27...h5, I only now realised that even I was still a long way away from a win.}  
(29... Qxb2 {Even this is not so bad}  30. f5 (30. Qd2 Qxd2 31. Rxd2 Nxf4 32. e5 
N6d5 {also does not look too convincing} ) 30... Ne5 31. Qd2 Qa3 32. Ra1 Qe7 {and while I suspect White should be technically winning, Black has a solid blockade for the moment, and with eight moves still to go till move 40 one never knows what could happen.} 
) 30. Qd3 {There is nothing better than to return a couple of pawns. Additionally, after blundering both mate on g2 and the knight on g3 in different lines, getting the queens off seemed like a very wise idea.}  
Nxf4 31. Qf3 Nxh3 32. Re2 { 32.Rf1 was also possible, but I liked the idea of doubling rooks on the d-file. }  
Qxf3 33. gxf3 Ng5 34. Kg2 Ne6 35. Red2 g6 {He has managed to set up a solid defence for the moment, but the following knight manoeuvre is surprisingly strong.}  
(35... g5 {[%cbm He should perhaps have tried]immediately, as Nf5 and Nd6 can be met by ...Re7. Practically speaking, Black certainly has drawing chances.} 
) 36. Nf1 h5 {Since there is no good way to meet Ne3-c4 anyway, Mickey has to go for it after all.}  
37. Ne3 h4 38. Nc4 g5 39. Nxa5 g4 40. Nxb7 {I decided to be really greedy, as it seemed to me that his h3-g3 pair would not really be dangerous.}  
g3 41. Nd6 Rg8 42. Nf5 Nf4+ 43. Kh1 h3 44. a5 { So far so good. I have a lot of extra material, and he has no threats. Additionally, my a-pawn is running very fast.}  
N6h5 45. a6 Ne6 {Now however, I sank into thought and began seeing all sorts of ghosts.}  
46. a7 (46. Rd6 { This had been my original intention preventing ...Ng5 and game over I thought. But then I saw}  
Nhf4 {with the idea of ...Ne2, and it spooked me a lot more than it should have} 
 47. Nxg3 Rxg3 48. a7 {and game over, one would think? ... Rg8 Rd8 is easy enough. But then you start seeing lines like}  
(48. Rg1 Ng2 49. a7 Rg8 50. Rxc6 {is still completely winning, which I more or less realised during the game. But some doubts still remained: For what did I give up my knight!?} 
) 48... Ne2 $1 49. a8=Q $4 Rg2 {and once you find one line in which are suddenly mated, you start looking for other options.} 
) 46... Ra8 47. Ra1 Ng5 48. Nh4 {So this is the idea that I settled on. I have to remove the knight from its best post on f5, but I could still not see any ideas for Black. }  
(48. Rd3 Nf4 49. Re3 {would have been a very clean solution, were it not for}  
g2+ (49... Ng2 {was what I feared, but this is very easily solved by}  50. Re2 
Nf4 51. Nxg3 {and the rook is protected} ) 50. Kh2 Ne2 $1 {I naturally did not see this}  
51. Rxe2 Nxf3+ 52. Kg3 (52. Kxh3 g1=Q 53. Rxg1 Nxg1+ {and I lose both rooks} ) 
52... Rxa7 53. Rb1 g1=Q+ 54. Rxg1 Nxg1 {and he has suddenly regained all his material, even if not solving all his problems as the knight is rather stuck on g1.} 
) 48... Nf4 49. b4 $2 {Panic, confusion... I just decided to get rid of the pawns once and for all.}  
(49. Ra5 {would still have been a clean win, as Mickey pointed out to me after the game. I rejected it because of}  
c5 {but now}  50. b4 {which I somehow failed to consider works much better}  Ng6 
(50... g2+ 51. Nxg2 hxg2+ 52. Rxg2 Nxg2 53. Kxg2 cxb4 54. cxb4 { and with the c6-pawn gone, the pawns are unstoppable} 
) 51. Nxg6 Nxf3 52. Rg2 { and this works even here.} ) 49... g2+ {Because of Kh2/g1 Ng6, I am forced to give up a full rook.}  
50. Nxg2 hxg2+ 51. Rxg2 Nxg2 52. Kxg2 Ne6 53. c4 Nc7 { And the pawns are reasonably well blockaded. It still felt like I should be winning, as his pieces are very passive and my king and f/e-pair will manage to create something before his king can get to the queenside.}  
54. Kg3 Kg6 55. Kf4 Kf6 56. e5+ Ke7 (56... Ke6 57. Ke4 f6 {would have been very logical, seemingly exchanging off the e5-pawn without letting my king take up a dominating position on e5. But I had seen in advance that it would fail to}  
58. Kd4 {and since he does not have the e6-square for the knight, my king decisively penetrates on the queenside.} 
) 57. Ke4 f6 {Quite logical, but not the best try.}  (57... Kd7 58. f4 Kc8 59. 
f5 Kb7 60. e6 {with the idea of fxe6 f6 and}  f6 61. Kd4 {But I missed}  Kb6 $1 {stopping Kc5 and making a draw. My father told me after the game that a Norwegian supercomputer gave me +4 even after 57...Kd7, so I guess I'm still winning. Am I going to find out how, or stick to the old cliché that this would have given better chances for a draw, but should still lose? The latter obviously. Deal with it.}  
(61... Na6 62. Rxa6 {seemed to win still.}  (62. Rxa6) ) ) 58. f4 fxe5 59. Kxe5 
Ne8 60. f5 Nd6 { Now I'm winning by force.}  (60... Nf6 {and the win, while attainable, is still not quite easy.} 
) 61. f6+ Kd7 62. Rd1 Re8+ 63. Kd4 {The point. While breaking the pin does not look logical, I am threatening Kc5 and after the forced.}  
Kc7 64. Re1 {White wins a crucial tempo}  Nf5+ {As Peter and I agreed on after the game, resigning instead of trying this would have been unprofessional.}  
65. Kc3 (65. Kc5 {Fortunately I had a lot of time...}  Rxe1 66. a8=Q Re5#) 65... 
Ra8 66. f7 1-0

